course: dictionary
lesson: 4578
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: The word help can be a verb, a noun, or a shout. Its meaning depends on the words around it, which is its context. In «Older children in big families often help a lot», help is a verb: they make work easier for others.
explain: In «Leaving a group can feel scary, but trusted adults can help», help means to make things easier or better for someone. When someone shouts «Help!» on its own, it means they are in danger, so the word works as an exclamation.
explain: «I can't help laughing» means you cannot stop yourself. A helping is one serving of food on a plate. Ask yourself which meaning fits the sentence, then swap your meaning in and check it still makes sense.
fact: Help can be a verb (to help), a noun (the help you get), or an exclamation (Help!).
fact: «Help!» is shouted when someone is in danger, and you should call for help in an emergency.
fact: In the UK the emergency number is 999. In the USA and Canada it is 911.
fact: «Help yourself» means take what you want, such as food on a table.
fact: «Assistance» and «aid» are more formal words for help, as in «Assistance was rendered by two volunteers.»
exercise: «Older children in big families often help a lot.» In this sentence, what does help mean? | A serving of food on a plate | A shout when someone is in danger | To stop yourself laughing | *To make work easier for others
exercise: «Help!» is shouted when someone is in danger. What kind of word is it here? | An adjective | A noun meaning a serving | A verb only | *An exclamation
exercise: Jayden says «I can't help laughing» means he is trying hard to laugh. What does it really mean? | He is helping someone else laugh | *He cannot stop himself laughing | He is trying to laugh | He wants to be funny
test: In «Have a second helping of pasta», what does helping mean? | A shout of danger | To stop yourself from doing something | *A serving of food | To make work easier
test: When schoolwork feels too hard, who should you ask for help? | *A trusted grown-up such as a parent or teacher | Your pet | Nobody, you must do it alone | A stranger in the street
exam: Which everyday word means the same as «assistance»? | Lunch | Sleep | *Help | Silence
